Welcome to the Zendesk kit. This kit is designed to help you understand, query, and make the most of the audit logs generated by Zendesk.

This playbook serves to get you oriented around the kit and provide some anchors into the data and dashboards.

# Getting Data In

Zendesk exposes its account audit log through the [Audit Logs API](https://developer.zendesk.com/api-reference/ticketing/account-configuration/audit_logs/) at `GET /api/v2/audit_logs.json`. Audit log access requires a Zendesk Enterprise plan (or the relevant add-on) and an administrator credential or API token.

The API returns a paginated `audit_logs` array. Poll it on a schedule (cursor-based pagination with `filter[created_at]` bounds works well), and ingest **each element of the array as its own entry** into the `zendesk-audit` tag. A Gravwell flow, a small script against the HTTP ingester, or any JSON-capable poller works. Set the entry timestamp from the `created_at` field.

# Overview

Each Zendesk audit log entry is a JSON object with the following fields:

- `id` - unique id of the audit event
- `action` - type of change: `create`, `update`, `destroy`, `login`, or `exported`
- `action_label` - localized display string for the action
- `actor_id` / `actor_name` - the user or system that initiated the change (`actor_id` is `-1` for system actors)
- `source_id` / `source_label` / `source_type` - the item that was changed; `source_type` values include `user`, `rule` (automations, macros, triggers, and views), `apitoken`, `ticket`, and `zendesk/app_market/app`
- `change_description` - human-readable description of the change
- `ip_address` - source IP of the actor (may be null for system-initiated changes)
- `created_at` - when the event occurred
- `url` - API URL of the audit record

# Field Extraction

This kit ships a JSON autoextractor bound to the `zendesk-audit` tag, and queries reference fields through the `ax` module, for example:

```
tag=$ZENDESK_AUDIT ax action=="destroy" source_type=="rule" actor_name source_label
| table actor_name source_label
```

# Queries
Queries are broken out into 2 types and have appropriate labels applied for reference.

## Stats
Primarily used by dashboards to generate metrics and provide an overview of activity within the Zendesk account.

## Detections
Queries built to detect specific behavior that can be indicative of malicious activity: account data exports, business rule and user deletion, API token lifecycle events, marketplace app changes, bursts of destructive or scripted changes, actors operating from multiple IPs, and login spikes. These can be used ad hoc, serve as a basis for your own detection engineering, or enabled as scheduled searches for alerting. Threshold-based detections should be tuned to your environment.

# Scheduled Searches
Every detection ships with a matching scheduled search that runs hourly over the previous two hours of data. The overlapping window accounts for API polling delay, so a threshold detection may alert twice for the same burst. They are **disabled by default** - review the thresholds, then enable the ones you want from the Scheduled Searches page.

# Dashboards
2 dashboards are provided in this kit.

## Zendesk - Audit Overview
High-level view of audit activity. Event volume over time by action, action and source type distribution, top actors, and recent audit events. Useful as the landing page.

## Zendesk - Account Security
Security-focused view: logins and destroy actions over time, data export events, top source IPs, business rule changes, and API token creation.

# Actionables
## Zendesk - Investigate IP Address
When an IP address appears in search results, an option under the "Zendesk" menu will run the IP investigation template against Zendesk audit logs.

# Templates
- **Zendesk - Investigate Actor** - audit activity for a user or system actor
- **Zendesk - Investigate IP Address** - audit activity from an IP address
- **Zendesk - Investigate Audited Item** - audit history for an item by name

# Macros
One configuration macro controls which tag the kit queries.

- `$ZENDESK_AUDIT` (default `zendesk-audit`)

If your tag differs from the default you must update **both** the macro value **and** the `Tag` on the `zendesk-audit` autoextractor (Extractors page) - the kit's queries reference fields through the autoextractor, so if it is not bound to your tag, no fields will be extracted and every query in the kit will return empty results.
